<p>Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney said Air Canada chief executive Michael Rousseau showed &quot;a lack of judgment&quot; and &quot;a lack of compassion&quot; by issuing an English-only video message after Sunday’s deadly incident at New York’s LaGuardia Airport. The criticism quickly widened beyond the airline’s response itself, as the absence of French in a national carrier’s public message revived a long-running debate in Canada over linguistic inclusion and the status of French, especially in Quebec. Critics said the controversy reflected the symbolic expectations placed on national institutions in a bilingual country, turning a condolence message into a broader dispute over representation and inclusion.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/2C-vznTo4MdyAX6Ua4Bx7q0yxdgKMT3LBrCk0MJAsDte7T3LMq-TubJ4POVDxG3M_jMid2E6K9r4XLdXyjTq8Z6mhbaoMN20OAV5gJNDAd0GGaz3J8TOFKp9xpZJL14E7ZDzzbUmSe3GlNfjYzt4q-7rq6rCJtEm5D2yhHQX4RqX_APPtscdpMlOsq8oIy9M' alt='Air Canada chief executive Michael Rousseau in 2024.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Resignation calls: The Guardian reported that Rousseau’s latest message prompted renewed calls for his resignation.</li><li>Personal language scrutiny: The Straits Times noted that critics focused on Rousseau himself as well as the video, emphasizing that he spoke almost no French.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Mark Carney: Carney publicly criticized the English-only condolence message, saying it fell short of the compassion and judgment expected after a deadly incident. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/prime-minister-carney-air-canada-ceos-english-condolences-131402465'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Critics in Quebec: Critics argued the message reflected a broader failure to respect French in a province where language rights remain a defining political issue. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/25/mark-carney-air-canada-fire-truck-crash'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Air Canada leadership debate: The 